WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
south africa  is struggling dozens of people have died  and more than 2 000 have been arrested  and countless businesses  have been ransacked but what has been  going  on exactly in south africa  [Music]  it all started with the arrest of the  former president jacob zuma  i'm not scared of doing  off going to jail for my beliefs  he was arrested at the start of july  after being sentenced to a 15-month  jail term for refusing to take part into  an inquiry  into corruption during his 10-year  presidency  he was forced from office by his own  party after mounting evidence of rampant  corruption in his administration  including claims his cabinet  appointments and lucrative contracts  were influenced by family and friends  his family and supporters called for a  national shutdown  and groups on whatsapp circulated voice  notes  urging people to take to the streets but  these protests transformed  into something bigger the unrest fueled  by frustration  in a country where millions of people  live life  on the edge  what started as political unrest has  swiftly turned into violence  looting and rioting  [Music]  and what about zuma are people doing  this for zuma  i think there's no there is no food more  than half of south africa's 60 million  people  are living in poverty with an  unemployment rate  pushing 40 percent an economic crisis  job cuts youth unemployment and of  course a global pandemic  have worsened the situation across the  country  and these protests suggest that many  have had enough  in short a lot of damage  thousands of people have stolen food  electronics  alcohol and clothes from shops and malls  in the provinces  of kwazulu-natal and halting  vehicles have been burnt and businesses  vandalized  with debris left strewn across the roads  dozens of people have been killed in the  resulting violence  some were trampled to death in stampedes  as people looted shops  police have arrested more than 2 000  people  and they've used stun grenades and  rubber bullets against the looters  but they seem powerless to actually stop  the unrest  highway of death gulf war that's what it  looks like down here if you carry on  further down the road  the car's burnt out red cars on the road  they've looted everything the  factories are burning warehouses are  burning the military  have been ineffectual so far they carry  live ammunition  but they are not permitted to use it  it's difficult to see how the  authorities are going to get a grip on  this situation  the violence and the unrest spread  quickly here  and there doesn't seem to be an end in  sight  we will restore calm and order so that  we can get on with the task of  rebuilding this country  the country's president cyril ramaphosa  has pleaded for calm  and he's deployed south africa's  military on the streets  but another politician minister kobutsu  chaveni  told sky news that youth unemployment is  a ticking  time bomb that is going to explode  and there are very real concerns that  the rioting and the unrest  will lead to widespread food shortages  people in durban  now have to queue up for hours to access  a handful of shops  that have opened their doors and people  here have told us  they worry the looters will soon turn up  here and shut this supermarket down

